    "texte": [
      "During a televised debate on youth mental health, Dr. Eleanor Vance, a clinical psychologist, confronted Arthur Sterling, a venture capitalist, regarding the modern obsession with achievement. 'Look around you, Arthur,' Dr. Vance began earnestly. 'From primary school onward, young people are conditioned to believe that their self-worth is entirely contingent upon measurable success. If they fail to secure top grades or prestigious internships, they feel utterly inadequate. Consequently, anxiety and burnout have reached epidemic proportions.'",
      "Arthur sighed, leaning forward before responding. 'I do not deny that pressure exists, Eleanor. However, competition has always been the primary engine of human innovation. If we stopped encouraging excellence, society would inevitably stagnate. Ambition pushes individuals to surpass their perceived limitations and achieve extraordinary things that benefit everyone.'",
      "'There is a vast difference between healthy ambition and toxic perfectionism,' Dr. Vance countered sharply. 'When society promotes the idea that one must succeed at all costs, ethical boundaries are easily compromised, and failure is stigmatized as a moral flaw rather than a natural step in learning. Moreover, this relentless pursuit of status often isolates individuals, turning peers into rivals.'",
      "Arthur paused thoughtfully, acknowledging her point. 'Perhaps the issue lies in how narrowly we define success today. In other words, we tend to equate achievement strictly with financial wealth and public recognition. Even though economic drive remains vital, I would agree that our culture ought to value resilience, empathy, and personal fulfillment just as highly as material triumph.'"
