"texte": [
"For generations, the relentless pursuit of a higher standard of living has been treated as an unquestioned virtue, synonymous with human progress. Economists measure national success through Gross Domestic Product, while individuals strive for larger homes, faster vehicles, and cutting-edge luxuries. Unquestionably, raising living standards has lifted millions out of destitute poverty, eradicated deadly diseases, and expanded access to education. Nevertheless, once basic material needs are comfortably met, does continually striving for more actually enhance our quality of life?",
"Psychologists refer to this dilemma as the 'hedonic treadmill.' As our income and material comforts rise, our expectations adapt almost instantaneously, causing the initial thrill of a purchase to evaporate. Consequently, people find themselves working harder merely to maintain a baseline of luxury that no longer brings genuine joy. Furthermore, this perpetual upward striving frequently comes at the expense of non-material assets—such as community engagement, mental tranquility, and ecological sustainability.",
"On the other hand, critics of economic stagnation warn that abandoning the drive for improvement could lead to complacency and reduce funding for vital scientific and medical research. Even though material excess can be counterproductive, human curiosity and the desire to solve complex problems naturally propel societies forward. The challenge, therefore, lies in distinguishing between quantitative 'standard of living' and qualitative 'quality of life.'",
"Ultimately, societies must ask themselves whether endless material expansion remains a rational goal. If we were to redefine progress around well-being, leisure, and environmental harmony rather than sheer economic output, striving for a better life would take on a far richer, more sustainable meaning."
