"Consider, for example, the daily interactions within different households. Children raised in homes filled with books and complex dinner-table discussions enter primary school already fluent in the formal academic register favored by teachers. Conversely, students from disadvantaged backgrounds often experience a cultural disconnect between the language spoken at home and the expectations of the classroom, which can be misdiagnosed as a lack of aptitude.",
"Furthermore, family background shapes parental involvement and educational aspirations. Affluent parents frequently possess the flexible schedules and confidence needed to advocate for their children, request specialized support, or navigate complex university admissions. Meanwhile, working-class parents, despite valuing education deeply, may feel intimidated by academic authorities or constrained by demanding shift work.",
"Nevertheless, family origin need not become an inescapable destiny. When schools actively demystify academic expectations, provide universal early-childhood programs, and offer structured mentorship, they can effectively bridge the cultural gap and empower students from all backgrounds to thrive."
