"Professor Vance nodded respectfully before offering a sobering counterpoint. 'While I admire that ideal, Samuel, empirical data suggests we have placed an unrealistic burden on schools alone. Over the past three decades, university enrollment has expanded dramatically, yet wealth inequality has continued to widen. When labor markets undervalue essential jobs, housing becomes unaffordable, and tax systems favor inherited wealth, a diploma alone can no longer guarantee upward mobility.'",
"'You raise a crucial structural point, Helen,' Dr. Mensah replied thoughtfully. 'However, without quality education, disadvantaged communities are stripped of the very voice and analytical tools needed to challenge unjust economic policies. Education does not merely prepare workers for the economy; it cultivates critical citizens capable of demanding systemic reform.'",
"Synthesizing their exchange, Clara Moreau concluded that education is an indispensable foundation for equity, yet insufficient in isolation. Unless educational investment is paired with fair labor laws, affordable healthcare, and progressive economic policies, schools will continuously be asked to solve social inequalities they did not create."
