"Yet, a growing phenomenon of workplace disengagement—popularly termed 'quiet quitting'—suggests that this traditional bond between work and fulfillment is fraying. Anthropologist David Graeber famously coined the term 'bullshit jobs' to describe the proliferation of administrative and corporate roles that even the employees performing them secretly consider pointless. When workers feel alienated from the purpose of their labor or treated as disposable cogs in a bureaucratic machine, enthusiasm inevitably gives way to cynicism.",
"Furthermore, expecting a single career to fulfill all of our emotional and existential needs can place an unrealistic burden on employment. When corporations promote 'passion' as a substitute for fair compensation and healthy boundaries, employees risk emotional devastation whenever layoffs or organizational restructuring occur.",
"Nevertheless, work has not lost its capacity to inspire when structured around autonomy, mastery, and social utility. Even though we ought to cultivate identity and joy outside our professions—through art, community, and relationships—meaningful work remains an essential pillar of human dignity."
